        exp: "Rule: Under UCC § 2-611(1), a repudiating party can retract an anticipatory repudiation until his next performance is due UNLESS the aggrieved party has, since the repudiation: (a) canceled the contract, (b) materially changed position in reliance on the repudiation, or (c) otherwise indicated that he considers the repudiation final[cite: 41]. Merely soliciting price quotations or inquiring about market replacement costs without entering into a binding cover contract, incurring nonrefundable expenses, or notifying the repudiator of cancellation does not constitute a material change of position[cite: 41]. Because the aerospace plant took no binding action and sent no notification of finality prior to receiving the retraction on October 15, the supplier's retraction was legally effective and reinstated the contract (Option B)[cite: 41]. Option A is incorrect because soliciting bids without binding commitment is not a material change of position[cite: 41]. Option C is incorrect because repudiations in time-of-the-essence contracts remain retractable until reliance, cancellation, or suit[cite: 41]. Option D is incorrect because the right to retract is not absolute; it terminates immediately upon the non-breaching party's material reliance, cancellation, or suit[cite: 41]."
