        exp: "Rule: Under Restatement (Second) of Torts § 519(2) and the landmark rule of Foster v. Preston Mill Co., strict liability for an abnormally dangerous activity does NOT apply where the harm that results is not within the scope of the extraordinary risk that makes the activity abnormally dangerous[cite: 41]. The risk that makes pile driving abnormally dangerous is the danger of subterranean concussive shockwaves causing structural foundation collapse, cracking, or physical subsidence[cite: 41]. Frightening unusually nervous animals (such as minks or caged poultry) into stampeding by acoustic rumbles without structural damage is an idiosyncratic reaction of sensitive livestock, not the physical structural destruction that makes the activity abnormally dangerous[cite: 41]. Strict liability is inapplicable (Option B)[cite: 41]. Option A is incorrect because strict liability is not absolute; it is bounded by the scope of the abnormal risk[cite: 41]. Option C is incorrect because res ipsa loquitur is a negligence inference, not a principle of strict liability[cite: 41]. Option D is incorrect because administrative compliance does not immunize a defendant from strict liability where the harm falls within the scope of the risk[cite: 41]."
