        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: At common law, burglary requires: (1) breaking, (2) entering, (3) the dwelling house, (4) of another, (5) in the nighttime, (6) with the intent to commit a felony therein[cite: 41]. An actual breaking occurs when the actor applies physical force to move or displace any structural closure or barrier (such as turning an unlatched doorknob to retract a latch bolt and pushing open a closed door)[cite: 41]. However, completed burglary strictly requires an ENTRY—meaning that some part of the defendant's physical body (a hand, foot, or finger) or an instrument used to consummate the ultimate felony (such as a hook used to steal goods) must physically cross the interior plane of the threshold[cite: 41]. Inserting an instrument merely to effectuate a breaking does not count as an entry[cite: 41]. Because Defendant broke the door open but fled before any part of his body or a felonious instrument crossed the threshold, completed burglary failed[cite: 41]. Having the specific intent to commit burglary and executing an overt physical breaking constitutes completed attempted common law burglary (Option B)[cite: 41]. Option A is incorrect because entry was absent[cite: 41]. Option C is incorrect because attempted burglary is an aggravated felony that ranks above attempted larceny[cite: 41]. Option D is incorrect because Defendant progressed far beyond simple trespass into a felony attempt[cite: 41]."
