        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: Under UCC § 2-209(2), a signed agreement between merchants which excludes modification or rescission except by a signed writing cannot be otherwise modified[cite: 41]. HOWEVER, under UCC § 2-209(4), an attempted modification that does not satisfy the writing requirement of § 2-209(2) can operate as a WAIVER[cite: 41]. Furthermore, under UCC § 2-209(5), a party who has made an executory waiver cannot retract it if the retraction would be unjust in view of a material change of position in reliance on the waiver[cite: 41]. Once the seller fully performs by custom-coating the cable in fire-retardant plenum and delivering the goods in reliance on the buyer's oral request, the waiver becomes completely executed and irrevocable[cite: 41]. The buyer cannot accept the custom performance and then assert the no-oral-modification clause[cite: 41]. The wholesaler is entitled to the $4,000 (Option B)[cite: 41]. Option A is incorrect because it ignores the statutory waiver exception in § 2-209(4)[cite: 41]. Option C is incorrect because UCC § 2-209(1) abolished the pre-existing duty rule for sales of goods[cite: 41]. Option D is incorrect because no-oral-modification clauses are valid under the UCC, but subject to waiver[cite: 41]."
