        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: Under Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability § 17 and prevailing comparative fault principles, a plaintiff's failure to exercise reasonable care for his own protection (including a failure to follow workplace safety protocols or deploy auxiliary safety shields) is treated under COMPARATIVE FAULT[cite: 41]. It reduces the plaintiff's damages in direct proportion to his percentage of responsibility, but does NOT operate as a complete bar to recovery or an extraordinary superseding cause as a matter of law[cite: 41]. Failing to deploy a blast shield during routine pressure testing is a classic, foreseeable human safety lapse in an industrial workplace, not an extraordinary unforeseeable misuse that extinguishes the supplier's strict liability for placing a defectively manufactured component into commerce (Option C)[cite: 41]. Option A is incorrect because foreseeable workplace safety lapses are concurrent intervening forces, not superseding causes that bar liability as a matter of law[cite: 41]. Option B is incorrect because implied secondary assumption of the risk has been merged into comparative fault in comparative negligence jurisdictions[cite: 41]. Option D is incorrect because an employer's safety duties do not immunize a third-party manufacturer from strict products liability[cite: 41]."
