        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: The critical distinction between larceny by trick and false pretenses turns on whether the victim intended to transfer mere possession/custody or full legal title (ownership)[cite: 41]. Larceny by trick occurs when an actor obtains mere possession of another's personal property by means of an intentional false representation or deceit[cite: 41]. False pretenses occurs when an actor obtains TITLE to the property through an intentional false statement of material past or present fact with intent to defraud[cite: 41]. Here, the sales director signed a temporary custody release and handed over keys pursuant to a supposed transport bailment; the dealership intended to convey only temporary possessory custody for transport to a show, while retaining full legal title and ownership in itself[cite: 41]. Because Defendant acquired possession by fraud without acquiring title, the crime is LARCENY BY TRICK (Option C)[cite: 41]. Option A is incorrect because embezzlement requires that initial possession be acquired lawfully and without fraud[cite: 41]. Option B is incorrect because false pretenses requires the fraudulent acquisition of title[cite: 41]. Option D is incorrect because the fraudulent acquisition of possession is specifically defined at common law as larceny by trick[cite: 41]."
