        exp: "Rule: Under established proximate cause and eggshell-plaintiff doctrines (Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 435, 461), once a defendant's negligence causes an initial foreseeable type of physical hazard (here, dropping and rupturing a heavy industrial vessel, dispersing an airborne plume into an adjacent public recreational area), the defendant is legally liable for physical bodily injuries caused by the exposure[cite: 41]. Liability attaches even though the defendant could not foresee the exact physiological mechanism or the particular plaintiff's idiosyncratic bodily vulnerability[cite: 41]. The general danger of respiratory distress or physical harm from inhaling an aerosolized slurry is within the foreseeable scope of the risk created by dropping an autoclave (Option B)[cite: 41]. Option A is incorrect because idiosyncratic physical reactions to tortiously released airborne substances do not break proximate causation under the thin-skull rule[cite: 41]. Option C is incorrect because crane operators owe a duty of reasonable care to all foreseeable persons in the vicinity, not a limited licensee duty[cite: 41]. Option D is incorrect because crane hoisting sounds in ordinary negligence, not strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities[cite: 41]."
