        fp: "Defendant and an acquaintance agreed to rob an armored cash delivery courier during a scheduled bank pickup[cite: 42]. Defendant's role was to act as the armed gunman who would disarm the courier, while the acquaintance drove the getaway car[cite: 42]. On the morning of the planned robbery, Defendant woke up with severe moral remorse, threw his handgun into a river, and decided to abandon the enterprise[cite: 42]. Two hours before the scheduled heist, Defendant called the acquaintance and stated: 'I am not going to do this. I threw away my gun, I am quitting, and you should call it off too.'[cite: 42] The acquaintance replied, 'You're a coward, but I don't need you; I'll rob the courier myself.'[cite: 42] Defendant hung up and stayed home[cite: 42]. The acquaintance recruited an independent third party, drove to the bank, held up the courier with a shotgun, stole $200,000, and escaped[cite: 42]. The jurisdiction adheres to traditional common law rules of conspiracy and accomplice liability[cite: 42].",
