        fp: "A pedestrian was walking past an upscale metropolitan hotel[cite: 42]. An independent window cleaning company had been hired by the hotel to clean exterior windows on the twelfth floor[cite: 42]. In direct violation of municipal safety ordinances and industry standards, the window cleaning crew failed to rope off the public sidewalk below and failed to secure their motorized scaffolding rig with safety anchor tethers[cite: 42]. A heavy, metal-framed squeegee slipped from a cleaner's grasp and plummeted twelve stories, striking the pedestrian on the shoulder[cite: 42]. The impact fractured the pedestrian's clavicle[cite: 42]. An ambulance transported the pedestrian to an emergency department[cite: 42]. While undergoing an orthopedic reduction under local anesthesia, an attending physician carelessly injected five times the standard therapeutic dose of an intravenous anesthetic, causing acute cardiac arrest, prolonged cerebral hypoxia, and irreversible severe brain damage[cite: 42]. The pedestrian sued the hotel and the window cleaning company for all of his injuries, including the catastrophic brain damage[cite: 42].",
