        topic: "Mixed",
        fp: "Defendant wanted to break into an occupied, single-family residential home at night to steal valuable antique jewelry[cite: 42]. At 2:00 AM, Defendant approached the back patio carrying a heavy iron pry bar[cite: 42]. Defendant found a set of sliding glass patio doors that were closed and securely latched[cite: 42]. Defendant wedged the blade of the pry bar into the seam between the door frame and the latch bolt, exerting heavy physical leverage[cite: 42]. With a loud snap, the interior latch sheared off, and the sliding glass door slid open six inches on its track[cite: 42]. Before Defendant could reach his hands, feet, body, or tools across the plane of the threshold into the interior of the home, an interior security alarm sounded an audible 110-decibel siren[cite: 42]. Startled, Defendant dropped the pry bar on the exterior patio, turned around, and fled across the lawn without crossing the doorway[cite: 42]. Defendant was apprehended three blocks away[cite: 42]. The jurisdiction adheres strictly to traditional common law criminal definitions[cite: 42].",
